🟢 **Low**

**Circuit breaker exit does not log cycles completed, leaving no breadcrumb for how many cycles ran before the trip**

When the circuit breaker fires, the log message describes which provider tripped it and why, but it does not include the `cycles` counter at the time of exit. If a user later needs to diagnose whether the breaker fired on cycle 2 or cycle 10, the only record is the returned `WatchResult` object, which is only printed by `cmdWatch` after the function returns. If the process dies, is redirected, or the return value is dropped by a caller, there is no log-level record of cycles-completed-at-trip. The fix is one additional token in the existing log line.

*Suggested fix:*

```suggestion
if (state.recentPosts.length >= circuitBreaker.maxReviews) {
							const windowMin = Math.round(circuitBreaker.windowMs / 60_000);
							log(
								`ai-review watch: circuit breaker tripped — ${target.provider} posted ${state.recentPosts.length} reviews for ${owner}/${repo}#${pullNumber} within ${windowMin} min after ${cycles} cycle(s). This usually means fixes are being pushed faster than the reviewer can converge (see docs/post-mortem-review-loop-churn.md). Stopping — let the PR settle, then restart watch for a final confirmation pass.`,
							);
							return { cycles, reason: "circuit-breaker" };
						}
```

